function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. On which interval is the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)?$\n\nI. $[0,2]$\n\nII. $(1,2)$\n\nIII. $(-\\infty ,0]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[0,2]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $(1,2)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $(-\\infty ,0]$ is closed, but it is not a bounded interval in the form $[a,b].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44ecce21-550b-523f-b8b9-aeef7fbf3bae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263605} {:answer/id #uuid "72477dd1-b33d-54df-b872-6f36021971cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263990} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5449cc-5239-52bf-9b1a-7218973f90ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264184} {:answer/id #uuid "a810da3d-3505-5c14-82f7-8bf490141215" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264436} {:answer/id #uuid "ed55bf29-39a9-50eb-89ad-464d2079ee83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a810da3d-3505-5c14-82f7-8bf490141215" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264436} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ca2a258-074d-5301-bd96-9a6250fb613b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d91387a3-da34-5d28-af8d-36338cffabf0" :question/math-academy-id "q-131066" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2, & x<0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nI. $[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]$\n\nII. $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]$\n\nIII. $[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}2=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=0$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Consider $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ so the function $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Consider $[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ so the function is not defined at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175902 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "205c5518-c67a-5784-8cc7-55b42ce7fb0e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether the Extreme Value Theorem Is Applicable to a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f9c9d7d-cafe-5ca3-aeda-6ab3a8844c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339402} {:answer/id #uuid "466f898a-a17d-5ef2-9df7-de21d4467270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339753} {:answer/id #uuid "53dd7fea-deec-5659-abd2-913b366a2e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339904} {:answer/id #uuid "71573f37-88c6-5316-841b-9e7cbf18b8f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340135} {:answer/id #uuid "9acfcc52-3763-5eb4-9f6b-ab6d62822c30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53dd7fea-deec-5659-abd2-913b366a2e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339904} :answer-field/id #uuid "8811fa51-bfe6-5509-90af-f31705a35d36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbdd6305-e297-593d-89f4-fb7ada8ade14" :question/math-academy-id "q-73455" :question/problem "![](images/de/de39663db59e42565c7d69d8a44a4cb95f14af0f4946b77cfc42eac02f7d2786.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. On which of the following intervals is the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)?$\n\nI. $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$\n\nII. $[-\\pi ,0)$\n\nIII. $[1,\\pi ]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval  $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is closed,but the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-\\pi ,0)$ is not closed, so, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,\\pi ]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[1,\\pi ].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c85ec74-7261-5a84-b268-2cb5156ec3e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339237} {:answer/id #uuid "324afc9c-5fc7-5f92-8306-6b5f8eebe2c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339570} {:answer/id #uuid "696a6442-ff9a-551c-9da2-c842f797cf3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340065} {:answer/id #uuid "85e6b77c-a45a-52cf-b8c9-1652a4b8a948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340328} {:answer/id #uuid "facb3de9-0178-54cd-b07c-1c80bbb75fb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "324afc9c-5fc7-5f92-8306-6b5f8eebe2c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339570} :answer-field/id #uuid "217a3a4a-e905-59b0-918b-c92be80eb6b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "122f233b-5382-5fef-8fbd-2b8682266e28" :question/math-academy-id "q-73481" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{x}}{{x}^{2}+1}?$   \n\nI. $[-1,5]$\n\nII. $[0,2]$\n\nIII. $[2,5]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,5]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because $\\sqrt[4]{x}$ is not defined anywhere in the subinterval $[-1,0).$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The intervals $[0,2]$ and $[2,5]$ are closed, and the function is continuous on each of these intervals Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on each $[0,2]$  and $[2,5].$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a68717c-3c5f-58a5-9174-e8f45f3e1ef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339216} {:answer/id #uuid "336f07da-9df2-582d-80ca-e44d8eb292b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339585} {:answer/id #uuid "43af0409-9e51-5d17-a8fb-98b37bd00bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339722} {:answer/id #uuid "72581646-406e-5e01-9bba-7ad77de94142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340144} {:answer/id #uuid "8e33f721-3fb7-5e44-8644-c40ab2c07185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "336f07da-9df2-582d-80ca-e44d8eb292b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339585} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a35e850-44d5-5740-b0b8-70909d0b180d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5aba1141-c0a4-54c1-bd64-30eb78786676" :question/math-academy-id "q-73484" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}?$    \n\nI. $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$\n\nII. $[\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n\nIII. $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$  is closed, but the function is not defined on a part of the interval because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$    is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ is  closed, and the function is continuous on $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341990 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27cfa7a4-d6c1-5351-99b2-6635044d85f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339474} {:answer/id #uuid "65459a76-323b-5a88-8a2f-2e108e43ea11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340024} {:answer/id #uuid "88971863-2310-5e3f-ac5d-6e4e1adfb7df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340348} {:answer/id #uuid "91912873-03a1-524d-9ad0-7c617923af84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340433} {:answer/id #uuid "be62f3bd-2eeb-5b57-889c-ca65dbe099b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91912873-03a1-524d-9ad0-7c617923af84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340433} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcb8b636-417c-59ba-b9cc-599917ded547" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7b9b884e-eb8a-59c7-aa31-49f0025fb47f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73495" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ -1, & x<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nI. $[-1,1]$\n\nII. $[\\frac{1}{2},1]$\n\nIII. $[-1,0)$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[-1,1].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}({2}^{-x})={2}^{0}=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-1)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=0$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[\\frac{1}{2},1].$ In this interval, $f(x)={2}^{-x}$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[\\frac{1}{2},1].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, the interval $[-1,0)$, is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}]}
